te correctly.\n\n**Most serious:**\n1. **`npm run record` can absorb tampering into the lock** (`record.ts:59`). It always rewrites the lock at the end, even when it records nothing. I changed a holdout fixture: `relock` refused it, but `record --only none-such` (no Stripe key needed) accepted the change and the lock check came back clean.\n2. **The \"clone never reads fixtures\" check is a text search** (`invariants.test.ts:759`). It only looks for a quoted string containing \"fixtures\" in the clone's `.ts` files. A clone that writes `\"fix\" + \"tures\"`, or goes through a symlink, passes the test and could read all 1122 holdout answers when verify runs on the host.\n3. **Copying the clone back keeps symlinks** (`sandbox.sh:62`). A builder can create a link with Node (the guard only blocks the `ln` command). Once the link is back in the repository it points at the real holdout.\n4. **Deleting the whole holdout folder passes the integrity check** (`fixtures.ts:167`), on the host as well as in the sandbox. verify then reports \"0 of 0\" as a pass.\n5. **Two files that shape the results are not locked** (`fixtures.ts:115`): `breaker-round1.json` and `holdout-seed.json`. Changing the rule tags in the breaker file changes which rules show as verified, and the lock doesn't notice.\n\n**Claims and statistics:**\n- **Policy changes:** the fidelity report now says the diff policy was \"unchanged\" for batch 1. `measurements.md` says it changed. The report gets this from git history, which here is one squashed commit.\n- **Pre-registration:** no code checks that a plan was committed before its batch. In this repository the plans and all 946 batch fixtures arrive in the same commit. The commits cited as proof don't exist in the history.\n- **Saturation:** the report says \"No breaker round logged\", but a breaker round exists (`breaker-round1.json`, its entry in `timings.jsonl`, and 12 of 40 breaker cases failing).\n- **Unlogged looks:** batch 2 was scored at least three more times in other worktrees, none of them in `runs.jsonl`, so the look count is too low.\n- **Missing timings:** the costs and durations of builds B and C are said to come from `timings.jsonl`, but they aren't in it.\n- **\"349 substantive cases\"** should be 343: six cases ask for the item's current quantity and produce no proration line. The conclusion doesn't change (bounds 0.87 % at 95 %, 1.33 % at 99 %).\n- **ID renaming hides wrong values** (`json.ts:75`). The step that renames generated IDs before comparing also rewrites values like `charge_automatically`, so `charge_manually` compares equal to it.\n\n**Left out, because I couldn't show a defect:**\n- **Secrets:** no keys, emails, account IDs or session tokens in committed files. The account name is already replaced with \"AcmeCo\".\n- **The clone itself:** it computes its answers. I found no lookups and no special cases for probe inputs.\n- **Recorded noise:** none in any of the 1870 fixtures. That looks plausible, because every recording runs on a frozen test clock.\n- **The `audit/` folder:** the README cites it but it is missing. The README also says it is kept out of every copy an auditor sees, so this is probably deliberate.","ttft_ms":1406,"type":"result","duration_ms":915118,"uuid":"e94361c1-8da2-4d0a-a707-60f914d7c4de","ttft_stream_ms":996,"time_to_request_ms":32,"first_content_frame_ms":996,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}

